Problem 3

A thin, uniform ring of mass $m$ and radius $r$ rests on the horizontal top of a table. The ring is made of a single turn of wire whose resistance per unit length is $\lambda$. Under the table top there is a solenoid coaxial with the ring.

The dependence on time $t$ of the current flowing in the solenoid is given by the formula

$$I_s = \left\{\begin{array}{lll} 0 & \text{for} & t < 0, \\ I_0\frac{t}{T} & \text{for} & 0 \le t < T, \\ I_0 & \text{for} & t \ge T, \end{array}\right.$$

(this is ensured by a suitable electronic circuit to which the solenoid is connected).

a) Find the largest value $I_0$ $(= I_{0m})$ for which the ring does not yet jump up above the table top.

b) Assuming that $I_0 \gg I_{0m}$ (see part a)), determine the height to which the ring will jump.

In the solution take into account the following information:

(i) when the ring is placed (coaxially with the solenoid) at a small height $z$ above the table top, and the current flowing in the solenoid is $I_s$, then to a very good approximation the flux of the magnetic induction through the ring is given by the formula $\Phi = (a - bz)\,I_s$, where $a$, $b$ are positive constants;

(ii) at every point the magnetic field produced by the ring can be neglected in comparison with the field produced by the solenoid;

(iii) the influence of the motion of the ring on the current flowing in it can be neglected;

(iv) the parameter $T$ is so small that the distance travelled by the ring up to the moment $t = T$ is negligibly small;

(v) the table top is non-magnetic and non-conducting, and the solenoid is at rest;

(vi) effects related to radiation and the aerodynamic drag of the air can be neglected.

Give the numerical values of the required quantities for $m = 2.3\cdot10^{-3}\,\mathrm{kg}$, $r = 2\cdot10^{-2}\,\mathrm{m}$, $\lambda = 0.9\cdot10^{-2}\,\Omega/\mathrm{m}$, $a = 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{T\cdot m^2\cdot A^{-1}}$, $b = 10^{-2}\,\mathrm{T\cdot m/A}$, $T = 10^{-3}\,\mathrm{s}$, $I_0 = 10\,\mathrm{A}$, $g = 9.8\,\mathrm{m/s^2}$ (gravitational acceleration).
